#
# TEMPLATE for the bench rig config. Copy to <tree>/.session/rig.conf, which is
# gitignored, and fill in YOUR values. Every one below is a placeholder.
#
#     cp tools/bench/rig.conf.example .session/rig.conf
#
# From a git worktree, which has no .session/ of its own, point KICKOS_RIG at the
# main checkout's copy instead:
#
#     KICKOS_RIG=/path/to/main/checkout/.session/rig.conf tools/bench/bench.sh <board>
#
# Sourced by /bin/sh: plain KEY=value, no expansion you do not want taken literally.

# --- this box -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The session directory: it holds env.sh (the cross-toolchain environment the build
# needs) and receives logs/. Absolute, because a run started from a worktree must
# still find the one real session directory.
RIG_SESSION=/path/to/checkout/.session

# The tree built and flashed when the caller sets no TREE. Absolute.
RIG_TREE=/path/to/checkout

# A python carrying pyserial, for the Espressif capture on THIS box. Optional:
# empty means fall back to $PY as exported by env.sh.
RIG_PYBIN=

# --- consoles -----------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE entry per board whose console cable the board name alone does not identify, spelled
# as the by-id path or glob that resolves it. Address a cable by its own SERIAL: numbers
# renumber across a flash, and this bus carries FTDIs and CP210x cables belonging to other
# work.
#
# Any board's row may be pinned here, including the ones with a tracked default. Do that as
# soon as a second ST-Link or a second CH34x joins the bus.
#
# The key is the board name uppercased with dashes turned into underscores, e.g.
# esp32c6-wroom -> RIG_CONSOLE_ESP32C6_WROOM.
RIG_CONSOLE_RX72M=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-FTDI_FT232R_USB_UART_XXXXXXXX*
RIG_CONSOLE_PICOPI=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-FTDI_FT232R_USB_UART_YYYYYYYY*
RIG_CONSOLE_PIZERO2350=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-FTDI_FT232R_USB_UART_YYYYYYYY*
# Where one cable serves several boards, THE PIN IS THE BOARD'S, NOT THE CABLE'S, and an
# empty log looks exactly like a board that printed nothing:
#   picopi      GP0/GP1, UART0. FTDI RX on GP0.
#   pizero2350  GP4/GP5, UART1. FTDI RX on GP4; UART0 is not on the Pi-Zero header.
#   teensy41    LPUART6 ("Serial1"). FTDI RX on PIN 1; pin 0 is the board's own RX.
RIG_CONSOLE_TEENSY41=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-FTDI_FT232R_USB_UART_YYYYYYYY*
# f411disco's ST-Link is a V2 with no VCP, so its console is a separate FTDI rather than the
# probe's own VCOM (f302nucleo's V2.1 does carry one and needs no row here).
RIG_CONSOLE_F411DISCO=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-FTDI_FT232R_USB_UART_ZZZZZZZZ*
#
# SEVERAL ROWS MAY NAME ONE CABLE, as the two above do: a bench with fewer FTDIs than
# boards moves one between them. That is fine while only one of those boards is attached,
# and wrong the moment two are: the pattern resolves for both and the capture is of
# whichever board happens to be wired. Re-point the row when the cable moves.

# --- the bench host, when the boards are not on this box -----------------------
# BENCH_HOST in the environment is what SELECTS remote mode; leave it unset and the
# boards are taken to be here. These say only how to reach the host once selected; the
# host name stays out of the tree and lives in the environment.
RIG_BENCH_PORT=22

# The remote directory holding the shipped tree and the run outputs, relative to the
# remote $HOME. Required in remote mode.
RIG_REMOTE_ROOT=kickos_bench

# A python carrying pyserial ON THE BENCH HOST, absolute and NOT $HOME-relative: it
# is passed through as a literal, so a `$HOME` here would not expand. Required there
# for the Espressif boards only.
RIG_REMOTE_PYBIN=/home/USER/.local/share/uv/tools/esptool/bin/python

# --- ST-Link probes ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Only needed once MORE THAN ONE ST-Link is on the bus, which is when st-flash stops
# being deterministic; tools/flash-stlink.sh has the detail. Key is
# RIG_STLINK_<BOARD> with the board upper-cased and '-' as '_'. st-info --probe prints
# the serials next to the chip each one is attached to.
#RIG_STLINK_F411DISCO=30FF6C065647373735081143
#RIG_STLINK_F302NUCLEO=0675FF494849887767183343
